  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 320 AM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nUpper ridging will continue to dominate the upper air pattern \nthrough much of the upcoming week, with temperatures expected to \nmoderate back to well above average for midweek. In fact, widespread \nlower 80s are expected to return for both Thursday and Friday, or 20 \nto 25 degrees above average for late October. Normally this time of \nyear, temperatures this warm combined with gusty south winds would \nlead to increasing fire weather concerns. That said, modest moisture \nadvection boosts dewpoints back into the 50s for much of the week, \nand this will keep humidity elevated to 35-45% each afternoon. Fire \nconcerns are expected to remain generally low due to this, though \nsoutherly winds gusting as high as 35 to 45 miles per hour are \nexpected both Thursday and Friday afternoon. These stronger winds \nare in response to deep surface cyclogenesis across the lee of the \nRockies, as an upper trough begins to eject into the Plains by \nFriday. \n\nBy this weekend, the aforementioned surface low will begin to eject \neast across the Dakotas Friday night, dragging a cold front into the \narea Saturday morning. Recent guidance solutions have sped up the \nprogression of this front, and as a consequence limits both \ninstability and precipitation across the area. Should this earlier \nfrontal passage verify, little to no rain accumulations are expected \nacross the area along the front. Some threat for deformation \nprecipitation is then possible across western Nebraska behind the \nfront, though guidance has also begun to suggest this will largely \nstay off to the north of the area in closer proximity to the upper \nlow track. That said, some precipitation cannot be ruled out for \nnow, and trends will continue to be monitored. Slightly cooler highs \nin the 60s are expected for Sunday in the wake of the frontal \npassage.",
